# ============================================================================
# Veriply Web (Next.js) Environment Configuration
# ============================================================================
# This file references configuration from the root ../../.env file.
# DO NOT duplicate configuration here - all values inherit from root .env
#
# This file exists for:
# 1. Documentation of which variables Next.js uses
# 2. IDE/editor environment variable hints
# 3. Build-time variable reference
# ============================================================================

# INSTRUCTIONS:
# -------------
# Option 1 (Recommended): Use Docker Compose
#   - Docker Compose automatically loads ../../.env
#   - All variables available to Next.js container
#   - No need to create .env in this directory
#
# Option 2: Local Development (without Docker)
#   - Ensure ../../.env exists (copy from ../../.env.example)
#   - Next.js will load from root .env automatically
#   - OR: Create symlink: ln -s ../../.env .env.local (for Next.js convention)
#
# WARNING: Do NOT create a separate .env file here with different values!
#          This will cause configuration drift between services.

# ============================================================================
# WEB-SPECIFIC VARIABLES (all loaded from root ../../.env)
# ============================================================================
#
# The following variables are used by Next.js at build time and runtime.
# All values come from ../../.env - see that file for configuration.
#
# IMPORTANT: Next.js loads environment variables in this order:
#   1. .env.local (highest priority, not committed)
#   2. .env.[environment] (.env.production, .env.development)
#   3. .env
#
# For this project, we use the ROOT .env file only.

# Public Environment Variables (Exposed to Browser)
# --------------------------------------------------
# These variables are prefixed with NEXT_PUBLIC_ and are embedded
# in the client-side JavaScript bundle at build time.

# NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL        - Backend API URL (e.g., http://localhost:8000)
# NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL        - Frontend app URL (e.g., http://localhost:3000)
# NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_NAME       - Application name (e.g., Veriply)
# → Used by client-side code to make API requests

# Private Server-Side Variables
# ------------------------------
# These variables are only available on the server (API routes, SSR)
# and are NOT exposed to the browser.

# NEXTAUTH_URL               - NextAuth base URL (e.g., http://localhost:3000)
# NEXTAUTH_SECRET            - NextAuth session encryption secret [REQUIRED]
# → Used by NextAuth for authentication

# API Configuration
# -----------------
# API_HOST                   - Internal API host (optional, for SSR)
# API_PORT                   - Internal API port (optional, for SSR)
# → Used for server-side API calls

# Database Configuration (if using Prisma/direct DB access)
# ----------------------------------------------------------
# DB_HOST                    - PostgreSQL host (if needed for Prisma)
# DB_PORT                    - PostgreSQL port
# DB_NAME                    - Database name
# DB_USER                    - Database username
# DB_PASSWORD                - Database password
# → Usually not needed in Next.js (API handles DB), but available if required

# Environment
# -----------
# ENVIRONMENT                - Runtime environment (development, staging, production)
# DEBUG                      - Debug mode (true/false)
# → Runtime environment settings

# Observability (Optional)
# -------------------------
# SENTRY_DSN                 - Sentry error tracking DSN
# → Client-side and server-side error tracking

# ============================================================================
# NEXT.JS ENVIRONMENT VARIABLE LOADING
# ============================================================================
#
# Next.js will automatically load .env files in this order:
#   1. .env.$(NODE_ENV).local  (e.g., .env.development.local)
#   2. .env.local              (not loaded when NODE_ENV is test)
#   3. .env.$(NODE_ENV)        (e.g., .env.development)
#   4. .env
#
# For this project:
#   - We use ROOT ../../.env as the single source of truth
#   - Symlink or reference root .env to avoid duplication
#   - Docker Compose passes root .env variables to Next.js container
#
# Variable Access in Code:
#   - NEXT_PUBLIC_* → Available in browser: process.env.NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL
#   - Private vars  → Server-only: process.env.NEXTAUTH_SECRET
#
# ============================================================================

# USAGE EXAMPLES:
# ---------------
#
# Client-side (components, pages):
#   const apiUrl = process.env.NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL;
#   fetch(`${apiUrl}/api/bots`);
#
# Server-side (API routes, getServerSideProps):
#   const secret = process.env.NEXTAUTH_SECRET;
#
# ============================================================================
